.TH CCOL_CHANNEL_CREATE_WITH_MPROCS 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_channel_create_with_mprocs \- create a two-way ccol_channel with custom memory management
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_channel *ccol_channel_create_with_mprocs(size_t max_size,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function creates a ccol_channel, which holds two circular queues
inside, one for each direction: ccol_owner_to_workers and
ccol_workers_to_owner. The function creates both with the same
max_size, so the two directions have the same capacity.

The function takes the ID of the calling thread, and that thread becomes
the owner of the ccol_channel. Every later ccol_chan_ call compares the
ID of its own thread with that ID and routes itself from the result:
the owner sends to ccol_owner_to_workers and receives from
ccol_workers_to_owner, while every other thread does the opposite.
Those calls therefore need no direction argument.

The function allocates both queues below the channel, and the ccol_channel
struct itself, through mmgmt_procs. The ccol_channel keeps its own copy of
those procedures and frees itself through them, so the
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t of the caller does not need to live longer than
this call. A NULL value selects the default malloc and free, which is
what the ccol_channel_create() macro does.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_size     Maximum number of messages that each direction can hold.
             Because one circular queue holds each direction, the bound
             of ccol_circular_queue_create_with_mprocs(3) applies: at
             least 1, no larger than ccol_max_elem_count, and no larger
             than SIZE_MAX / sizeof(c_message_t).
mmgmt_procs  Custom memory management procedures, or NULL to use the
             default malloc and free. If the value is not NULL, all of
             malloc, calloc, realloc and free must be set.
err_str      Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure. Pass
             NULL to ignore it.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the function gives a pointer to the new ccol_channel. If
err_str is not NULL, the function sets *err_str to NULL.

On failure, the function gives NULL, and if err_str is not NULL,
*err_str describes the reason. A failure has one of three causes:
mmgmt_procs is malformed because it sets some but not all of malloc,
calloc, realloc and free; an allocation failed; or the function could
not create one of the two circular queues below the channel, for any of
the reasons in ccol_circular_queue_create_with_mprocs(3), such as a
max_size that is out of range. If the function creates the first queue
and then fails on the second one, it frees the first queue before it
returns, so no failure path leaves anything allocated.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations are thread-safe.
You must destroy the channel with ccol_channel_destroy() after you finish
with it.
The channel picks the direction itself from the ID of the thread that
calls it.
The owner thread is the thread that called this function, and it must
stay alive for all the time that the ccol_channel is in use. To route a
message, the channel compares the ID of the calling thread with the ID
that it took here. After the first owner exits, the OS can give the
same ID to a new, unrelated thread; the channel then routes that thread
as the owner too, and it reports nothing.
There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with a ccol_channel:
call fork(2) before you create the ccol_channel, or create the
ccol_channel, then call fork(2) and immediately call exec(3). One
pattern is not supported: a program that continues to run with no
exec(3) in between while a ccol_channel from before the fork is live.
On top of that rule, the library adds a defense in depth: the child
never inherits a lock in the locked state from another thread that
held it at the instant of the fork.  Because neither queue below the
channel owns a thread of its own, a ccol_channel that a child inherits
in that way stays fully usable.
.fi
